1wk5|a|Having the qualities of a speculum, or mirror; having a smooth, reflecting surface; as, a specular metal; a specular surface. ; Of or pertaining to a speculum; conducted with the aid of a speculum; as, a specular examination. ; Assisting sight, as a lens or the like. ; Affording view.	j:1owx,136,33;eau,69,3~1owx,136,33;eau,69,3
1wk6|n|To consider by turning a subject in the mind, and viewing it in its different aspects and relations; to meditate; to contemplate; to theorize; as, to speculate on questions in religion; to speculate on political events. ; To view subjects from certain premises given or assumed, and infer conclusions respecting them a priori. ; To purchase with the expectation of a contingent advance in value, and a consequent sale at a profit; -- often, in a somewhat depreciative sense, of unsound or hazardous transactions; as, to speculate in coffee, in sugar, or in bank stock. ; To consider attentively; as, to speculate the nature of a thing.	a:tsa,8,2~tsa,8,2
